$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}f(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}\dfrac{\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}\qquad\gray{\text{This is the rule for }x\neq 5}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}\dfrac{\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{x-1}+2}{\sqrt{x-1}+2}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}\dfrac{x-1-2^2}{(x-5)(\sqrt{x-1}+2)}\qquad\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}\dfrac{\cancel{x-5}}{\cancel{(x-5)}(\sqrt{x-1}+2)}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}\dfrac{1}{(\sqrt{x-1}+2)}
\\\\
&\small\text{(This is allowed because }x\neq 5)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{5-1}+2}\qquad\gray{\text{Direct substitution}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{4}
\end{align}$

We obtained that if we set $f(5)=\dfrac{1}{4}$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 5}f(x)=f(5)$, which makes $f$ continuous at $x=5$.

Since we already saw that $f$ is continuous for any other $x>1$, we can determine that it's continuous for all $x>1$.
